<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/intermediate/ancient-greece-and-rome">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Greece and Rome are the West's classical deposit — <strong>democracy, philosophy, drama,
    law, engineering and the very habit of systematic record-keeping</strong> — laid down between
    roughly 800 BCE and 476 CE. They are also a lesson in scale: Athens proved small states can
    punch intellectually far above their weight; Rome proved administration can hold a
    Mediterranean together for six centuries.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four inheritances, and how we got them" widget="equationStep" config={grCfg} />

  <h2>What a deeper dive reveals</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Greek science was real science:</strong> Eratosthenes measured Earth's circumference to within a few percent (~240 BCE) using shadows and geometry; Aristarchus proposed heliocentrism 1,800 years before Copernicus. The tradition lacked institutions, not ideas.</li>
    <li><strong>Rome's republic-to-empire arc is the model:</strong> institutions designed for a city-state failing as the state grew — civil wars (Caesar, 49 BCE) resolving into autocracy (Augustus). Every constitution-writer since has studied the failure mode.</li>
    <li><strong>Slavery was the foundation both ran on:</strong> Athens' democracy rested on enslaved labour; Rome's economy was slave-powered at scale. The classical "freedom" was citizenship's freedom, narrowly shared.</li>
    <li><strong>Transmission matters as much as creation:</strong> Arab scholars translated and preserved Greek texts (the House of Wisdom), returning them to Europe centuries later — the classics survived on a relay, not a straight line.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Thucydides, History of the Peloponnesian War; Mary Beard, SPQR; Ward-Perkins, The Fall of Rome.</p>
</TopicPage>
